## 17. Versioning & Canonical Identity Protection

Character identity continuity is a major product requirement.

Once a character is published and users establish conversations, avoid silently changing fundamental personality characteristics.

Separate configuration changes into distinct version categories.

### Persona version

Includes:

- Identity.
- Core personality.
- Fictional lore.
- Inner Compass.
- Core Anchors.
- Fundamental linguistic identity.

### Intelligence version

Includes:

- Conversational judgment.
- Contextual adaptation.
- Memory use.
- Response quality.
- Rendering guidance.

### Expertise version

Includes:

- Specialist methodology.
- Interpretive improvements.
- Technical knowledge.
- Data integration.

### Global configuration version

Includes:

- Global hard rules.
- Shared intelligence.
- Global soft defaults.
- Shared specialist guidance.
- Memory policies.
- Runtime defaults.

### Publication lifecycle

Support:

- Draft.
- Testing.
- Published.
- Archived.

Maintain immutable published snapshots.

Existing conversation threads should reference their established persona version.

Compatible intelligence improvements may be applied without changing the character's core identity.

Necessary safety and privacy updates must remain applicable to all active conversations.

### Identity locking

Once identity is locked, protect:

- Character ID.
- Display name.
- Fundamental identity.
- Core personality.
- Fictional lore.
- Inner Compass.
- Core Anchors.

Major personality changes require an explicit administrative release and migration decision.

Do not silently alter existing conversation identities.

### Version management features

Add:

- Compare versions.
- Restore compatible previous versions.
- Duplicate draft.
- Publish new version.
- View release notes.
- View affected characters.
- Audit history.
- Identify breaking changes.

Rollback must not restore obsolete safeguards or unauthorized access permissions.

